# The probability of the flip is the variable p, never assigned, so the bound is asked for
# every initial p.  Where p >= 2 the flip is reached with a probability outside [0, 1]: such a
# run is an error from which no bound may be proved, so against an upper bound it is worth +oo
# and the bound fails at every p >= 2.  Elsewhere c = 0 and the bound holds.
# Expected: unknown.  When an out-of-range probability made the run abort (worth 0 in both
# directions) the preexpectation was 0 at p >= 2 and the bound was proved (valid).
c := 0;
if p >= 2 {
	if flip(p) {
		c := 1
	}
} else {
	c := 0
}
return c
?Ex[c] <= 0.5
